# Template-free synthesis of hollow carbon-based nanostructures from MOFs for rechargeable battery applications

College of Chemistry and Materials Science, Guangdong Provincial Key Laboratory of Functional Supramolecular Coordination, Materials and Applications, Jinan University, Guangzhou 510632, China;   
2CAS Key Laboratory of Molecular Nanostructure and Nanotechnology, Beijing National Laboratory for Molecular Sciences, Institute of Chemistry, Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS), Beijing 100190, China;   
3University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China
Hollow carbon-based nanostructures (HCNs) have found broad applications in various fields, particularly rechargeable batteries. However, the syntheses of HCNs usually rely on template methods, which are time-consuming, low-yield, and environmentally detrimental. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed by organic ligands and inorganic metal nodes, have been identified as effective platforms for preparing HCNs without adding extra templates. This review summarized the recent progress in template-free synthesis of HCNs enabled by MOFs and their applications in rechargeable batteries. Different template-free strategies were introduced first with mechanistic insights into the hollowing mechanism. Then the electrochemical performances of the HCNs were discussed with highlight on the structure-function correlation. It is found that the built-in cavities and nonporous for HCNs is of critical importance to increase the storage sites for high capacity, to enhance charge and mass transport kinetics for high-rate capability, and to ensure the resilient electrode structure for stable cycling. Finally, the challenges and opportunities regarding MOFs-derived HCNs and their applications in rechargeable batteries were discussed.
hollow carbon-based nanostructures, template-free synthesis, metal-organic frameworks, rechargeable battery, electrode material
Citation: Bin DS, Zheng ZL, Cao AM, Wan LJ. Template-free synthesis of hollow carbon-based nanostructures from MOFs for rechargeable battery applications. Sc China Chem, 2023, 66: 65–77, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-022-1398-5
## 1 Introduction
Hollow carbon-based nanostructures (HCNs), featuring thin shells outside and intriguing cavities inside, promised high conceptual and technological potential for rechargeable batteries electrodes [1–4]. The built-in cavities for HCNs can not only provide effective zone to buffer the volumetric deformation for high structural stability, but also function as a robust container with suitable room for encapsulating the huge-volume-deformation electrode materials like Si, Sn, and S [5,6]. Furthermore, the HCNs with a thin and nanoporous shell would offer a shorter diffusion distance and accelerate the electrolyte infiltration to facilitate the charge/ mass transfer for fast electrode kinetics, accordingly promising outstanding cycling stability and high-rate capability [7–9]. Template-based methods, which necessitates a nanocasting procedure by coating the carbon precursor, like thermosetting polymers, onto the removable extra templates, have long been identified as the straightforward routes for HCN synthesis [5,6]. Such nanocasting protocol-involved multi-steps processes including templates synthesis, precursor coating, and templates removal are time-consuming, low-yield, environmentally detrimental, and challenged for the precise control of the structural parameters. Compared with the template-based method, the template-free routes always started from the direct synthesis of the carbon precursors with a control on the nanoparticles’ chemical property for HCNs. As the template-free methods did not require the previous synthesis of template, coating of precursors, and using hazardous agents to etch the template, accordingly it is efficient, environment-friendly, and easy to scale up [7–9]. Moreover, the features of HCNs synthesized by templatefree methods were not predetermined by the template particles, and thus we can build complex architectures and control the key structural parameters of the HCNs. Besides the methods, the frequently-used carbon precursors, like polymeric resin, are limited by their simple compositional chemistry, which leaves inadequate tunability in the design of HCNs [5,10,11]. Therefore, template-free synthetic strategies based on the precursors with a rich chemistry for HCNs with high level of complexity and functionality are highly desired [12].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which are constructed by carbon-containing organic ligands and inorganic metal nodes via coordination bonds, have long been identified as reliable precursors for porous carbon materials [13–16]. Distinguished from the traditional carbon precursors like polyme or biomass, MOFs showed various attractive characteristics and advantages [17–21]. (1) The uniform-distribution inorganic metal nodes in the frameworks could not only function as pore-makers or catalyst for catalytic graphitization, but also act as the metal sources for fabricating metal nanoparticle, single-atom metal, and metal-compound. This metal or compound could dispersedly incorporate in the carbon matrix. (2) The extraordinarily rich chemistry of the organic ligands provides enormous room to tune the physicochemical properties of the carbonized species, allowing a molecular level design of HCNs with desirable functionality. (3) The transformation of the MOFs into HCNs showed a considerable tunability in design of the key structural parameters like morphology, dimensionality, and porosity.
The rich chemistry for MOFs not only ensures the compositional diversity of the obtained HCNs to satisfy different applications, but also provides themselves the possibility to act as the effective template-free synthetic platforms for HCNs [20,22–25]. Over the past years, extensive research efforts have been devoted to exploring template-free synthesis methods for HCNs from MOFs. As a result, several template-free synthetize strategies based on different hollowing mechanisms have been proposed, including surface protecting [22,23,26], selective etching [24,27,28], ligand exchange [29,30], surface-stabilized contraction [25,31,32], and gas-induced inflation [33]. These emerged template-free strategies for MOFs-derived HCNs enable new perspectives in different applications, particular in rechargeable batteries, such as lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) [34,35], Li-S batteries [36,37], sodium-ion batteries (NIBs) [22,38], potassium-ion batteries (KIBs) [39,40], and zinc-air batteries (ZABs) [23,41]. The control of the structural parameters of the HCNs enabled by such template-free strategies is of critical importance to enrich the storage sites for high specific capacity, to enhance transport kinetics for high-rate capability, and to ensure the resilient electrode for outstanding cycling endurance [15,42,43].
In this review, the template-free synthesis strategies for HCNs from MOFs were introduced first with special attention on the hollowing mechanism. Then the electrochemical performances of the HCNs in various rechargeable batteries were discussed, such as LIBs, lithium-sulfur batteries, NIBs, KIBs, and ZABs. Finally, perspectives and challenges for the further development of MOFs-derived HCNs for rechargeable battery applications were proposed. We expect that this review will provide useful guidelines for the targeted design and template-free synthesis of HCNs from MOFs with a higher level of functionality for electrochemical applications, such as rechargeable battery, supercapacitor, and electrocatalysis.
## 2 Synthesis approaches
The key to a successful construction of the HCNs lies in how to create the cavities inside but keep the outside shell unchanged [44,45]. In general, the existence of intraparticle difference, for example, a core-shell typed difference, would make it possible to selectively remove the core part to create the inner void space by controlled etching or pyrolysis [5,11]. For the template-free synthesis of HCNs from MOFs, it is critical to control the chemical properties of the solid MOFs particles. Below, we discussed five template-free synthesis protocols for HCNs from MOFs with mechanistic insights into the hollowing processes, including surfaceprotected etching, selective etching, ligand exchange, surface-stabilized contraction, and gas-induced inflation.
## 2.1 Surface-protected etching
One of the most straightforward routes to create cavities in HCNs is performing the selective etching of the core part of a solid particle. Building a chemically robust surface by a surface-protecting agent makes the selective etching of the core portion possible [22,23,26,46]. Using Zn-based zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIF-8) as an example [22,47], Yamauchi and the co-worker [22] identified that after being treated by tannic acid (TA), ZIF-8 nanoparticles could form a passivation layer on the surface, which enabled the selective etching of the core part of the ZIF-8 nanoparticles (Figure 1a). Figure 1b showed a representative transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of the prepared ZIF-8 particles, which exhibited solid nanoparticles in center. A simple TA solution treatment of the ZIF-8 particles was able to create a cavity inside the nanoparticles, forming the desired hollow nanostructures (Figure 1c). A high temperature pyrolysis process was able to transform the hollow MOFs into HCNs with morphological characters well inherited (Figure 1d). It is noted that identifying the tannic acid as suitable agent is critical to form the surface-protected layer. Other polymeric acids like polymaleic acid could also selectively etch ZIF-8 for the cavity but result in the undefined structures, which is probably because it was not able to attain uniform coating of linear polymaleic acid. The polymeric acids, which cannot provide enough protons, like hyaluronic acid, will not be able to etch the core part of ZIF-8 [22].
Additionally, Liu et al. [26] demonstrated that the TAtreated ZIF-67 could also result in similar hollow MOFs for the synthesis of Co@N-Doped HCNs. Figure 2a illustrated the proposed surface-protected etching mechanism. The TA was able to simultaneously act as both a surface protecting agent and an etching agent. The rich hydroxyl group and planar conformation of the TA molecule make them facilely adsorbed on the surfaces of the ZIF-8 or ZIF-67 nanoparticles to form a chemically robust layer. The large-size TA molecules, which cannot penetrate the particle, uniformly covers on the MOFs surface. On the other hand, the TA would release protons $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+})$ , which were able to penetrate though the MOFs surface to the core region, thus dissolving the core part of ZIFs to form a cavity inside. Notably, only a short duration of TA treatment (e.g., 2 min) could selectively remove the central part of the ZIF-67 particles to form the hollow architecture.
It is noted that such a surface-protected etching strategy could also be applicable for other MOFs. For example, Xu et al. [23] reported that treating a MOF named as FeNi-MIL88B with 2,4,6-tris(4-pyridyl)pyridine (tpy) in hot $N,N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) would produce the hollow capsular nanoparticles (Figure 2b). The tpy would anchor on the uncoordinated $\mathrm{Fe(III)/Ni(II)}$ sites of FeNi-MIL-88B surface which would drastically enhance the chemical stability of the surface layer. As a result, the hot DMF could selectively etch the core and produced a hollow structure.
## 2.2 Selective etching
A revisit on the surface-protected etching protocol revealed that the key for a successful construction of HCNs lies in the formation of a core-shell typed intraparticle difference, where the inner core is much more chemically fragile than the exterior shell, making it possible for the selective etching of core to create a cavity inside. From a synthesis point of view, it becomes reasonable for us to expect that a hollow particle could be directly created if there exist such an inhomogeneous nature of inner composition [8]. However, MOF is crystal substance, which was always considered naturally homogenous integrality. Designing crystal particles with inner compositional inhomogeneity is desired but challenged [24]. Interestingly, Huo and co-workers [24] successfully created inhomogeneous MOFs (MIL-101) particles, based on which they developed a facile selective etching protocol for the construction of complex multilayered hollow MOFs. As illustrated in Figure 3a, the inhomogeneous MOF nanoparticles were synthesis via a hydrothermal reaction with 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC)}$ and chromium ions as organic linker and metallic node, respectively. In such solid MIL-101 nanoparticles (Figure 3b), the shelled layer is chemically more hardened than the core part, providing the possibility to selectively etch the vulnerable core to produce hollow nanostructure after an acetic acid treatment (Figure 3c). Impressively, with the inhomogeneous MIL-101 nanoparticles as seeds, an expansion of the seeded growth route through repeated crystal growth and then performing the acetic acid treatment at a time could produce the multi-shelled hollow MOFs (MSHMs) (Figure 3d). The authors showed that a systematic control of the MOF nucleation and crystallization endows the capability in controlling the key structural parameters of the hollow MOFs, including the precise control of the cavity size and the shell thickness.
A surface-energy-driven process could well explain the formation of inhomogeneous nature of the single MIL-101 nanoparticles. At the preliminary stage of coordinating reaction, a large number of nano-crystals with lower crystallinity quickly produced and aggregated to form a spheric core to minimize the surface energy. After such an explosive nucleation stage, the quantity of metal ions/clusters and organic linker sharply declined to a concentration lower than the critical concentration of nucleation, resulting in the stop of additional nucleation. Meanwhile, as the crystal continues, the newly formed MOF crystals with low surface energy on the surface layer have higher crystallinity. On the contrary, the embedded core pare is composed of aggregate nanocrystals with more defects. As a result, the core part is chemically more fragile than the outside layer.
Besides MOFs, the design of inhomogeneous nature of the compositional distribution inside a single nanoparticle via controlling the growth reaction kinetics could also be expanded to other systems, including resin polymer, silica, and coordination polymers [7,8,48,49]. For example, we identified that a control on the growth kinetics of 3-aminophenolformaldehyde (3-AF) resin nanospheres was able to induce certain compositional inhomogeneity inside these produced nanoparticles, where the shell showed a much higher polymerization degree when compared with the inner core (Figure 4) [7]. A treatment by acetone aqueous solution of the collected 3-AF nanospheres was able to selectively etch the core of the nanoparticles to form the cavity. The complicated hollow structures, particularly multishelled hollow nanospheres, could be produced by such a highly reliable synthesis route [4,8]. It is noted that the identification of a suitable solvent to probe the innate inhomogeneity of the nanoparticle is one of the most critical steps for selective etching of the core part, because a solvent with lower dissolving capability remains unaffected on the particles while a solvent with a much stronger dissolving capability will excessively dissolve them [8].
## 2.3 Ligand exchange
The above-discussed synthesis routes for HCNs from MOFs relied on an etching of core-shell typed particles. They required not only a precisely design of the MOFs nanoparticle for a core-shell typed inhomogeneous configuration but also a suitable solvent to selectively remove the core part. It is highly desired that the syntheses were free from the etching procedure. Considering that MOFs were assembled by carbon-containing organic ligands and inorganic metal nodes via the coordinate bonds, researchers devoted their efforts and identified that ligand exchange turned out to be an effective mean to construct hollow MOFs for HCNs. In the ligand exchange process, the dissolution of the preexisted MOFs is much faster than rebuilding of new nanocrystal, thus making it possible to creat cavities inside MOF, which could transform into HCNs after pyrolysis [29,30].
## 2.4 Surface-stabilized contraction
The selective etching protocol for HCNs reminded us that a suitable etching agent was able to probe the internal complexity of an inhomogeneous core-shell typed particle. Actually, a pyrolysis treatment would also result in the different evolution of the core and shell part when the thermal stability of core part and shell layer is distinguished [25,31,32]. For example, Qiu et al. [25] identified that a simple pyrolysis of a MOFs@MOFs particle of ZIF-67 nanolayer coated on the surface of ZIF-8 seeds (ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67) would result in HCNs. Figure 6a schemed the hollowing process of surfacestabilized contraction, which was proposed based on the fact that ZIF-67 is thermally less stable than ZIF-8. When the pyrolysis temperature rose up to $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the shell layer of ZIF-67 would decompose to form a rigid CoO/carbon shell, which has an outward adhesive force on the core-shell interface. When the temperature further increases, e.g., to $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , such an outward adhesive force would suppress the inward contraction of the ZIF-8 core due to the thermally induced polycondensation. Then cavities emerged in the center as a balance of the mass loss induced by the releasing gas of small molecules. With the temperature rising to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the Zn component from ZIF-8 evaporated and HCNs could be obtained.
Due to the reliable synthesis capability of such surfacestabilized contraction protocols, complex HCNs like multishelled HCNs, could be prepared via such a surface-stabilized contraction protocol. Shen and the Co-workers reported the prepared multishelled HCNs and yolk@multishelled structures of metal/nitrogen/carbon composites via such a surface-stabilized contraction [31]. As schemed in Figure 6b, multilayer solid ZIFs (ZIF-8/ZIF-67) were designed by a layer-by-layer crystal growth process, which was able to well control over the shell number and composition. After a simple pyrolysis, the designed ZIFs with layer-on-layer structure transform into HCNs from one shelled structure to multishelled structure based on the designed precursors (Figure 6c). As simple as it may look, the construction of MOF@MOF is not an easy task, because it usually not only requires these MOF components to possess the same crystal structure with similar cell parameters but also needs a delicate control of the MOF snythesis [31]. It is noted that such a surface-stabilized contraction could also work when coating polydopamine (PDA) and agarose (AG) biomass on the surface of ZIF-8. Yamauchi et al. [50,51] identified that the interesting spontaneous hollowing process would happen for core-shell composites of ZIF- $8@$ PDA and ZIF-8@AG during the carbonization.
## 2.5 Gas-induced inflation
The above discussed template-free synthesis is effective for HCNs without using template. However, these protocols necessitate either a delicate design of the MOFs compositions or the multistep operations to create the cavity like surface protection, ligand exchange, and selective etching. Researchers thus tried to explore new synthesis for HCNs from MOFs based on more straightforward and simpler operations. Xu et al. [33] identified that it was possible to directly construct the porous carbon with built-in hole through the pyrolysis of MOFs based on a released gasinduced inflation process. As illustrated in Figure 7a, Znbased solid triazole-rich MOF nanoparticles doped with Co and/or Fe ions were designed as precursors (Figure 7b). After a one-step pyrolysis treatment by heating the MOFs to a high temperature, for example $1{,}000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the three-dimensional carbon materials with cavities inside were produced (Figure 7c). Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) revealed a sharp mass loss (around $60\mathrm{\textperthousand}$ ) occurred at around $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , suggesting intense gas release induced inflation of the MOFs nanoparticles for the hollowing.
It is noted that such a released gas-induced inflation route for HCNs was also found applicable for objects other than MOFs, such as melamine formaldehyde resin. In our previous study, we found that a simple pyrolysis of a commercially available melamine-formaldehyde (MF) resin would result in the spontaneous hollowing process for HCNs [9]. A novel released gas-induced glass-blowing mechanism was response for the self-hollowing of the carbon structure. As illustrated in Figure 8a, the MF resin foam with abundant methylene ether groups $(-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{-O-CH}_{2}-)$ linkage was selected as the carbon precursor. A two-step structural transformation in the pyrolysis produced the cavity inside the carbon blocks. The initial low-temperature heating stage scissored the ether linkage to soften the MF resin and make it highly deformable. Subsequently, the rising of the temperature led to the fast decomposition of the resin with intense gas release (Figure 8b), which effectively inflated the softened building blocks of the MF foam into a hollow spherical architecture (Figure 8c). Notably, this simple synthetic protocol needing only simple pyrolysis of the MF resin is of high potential for large-scale HCNs production.
## 3 Rechargeable battery applications
Rechargeable batteries have found broad applications in different fields including portable electronics, electric vehicles, and large-scale energy storage [52–58]. One of the most critical points for building reliable rechargeable batteries lay in seeking the advanced electrode materials, because the electrochemical performances of the rechargeable batteries are mainly dependent on the electrodes including both anodes and cathodes [59–61]. Accordingly, over the past several decades numerous research efforts have been devoted to the development of high-performance electrode materials for rechargeable batteries [62–65]. Engineering carbon-based materials into hollow architectures has witnessed numerous successes in the development of high-performance cathode and anode materials [66–71]. HCNs featuring high specific area and thin shelled in principle could promise more storage sites and faster electrode kinetics [72–74]. Meanwhile, the built-in cavities were able to buffer the volumetric deformation to hinder the electrode disintegrate for the superior structural stability and cyclability [46,59,73]. Moreover, for MOFs-derived HCNs, the metal nodes in the framework could not only act as catalyst for graphitization or pore-makers, but also provide the metal sources for fabricating the composite of carbon/metal or carbon/metal-compound, which could deliver much higher specific capacity than the pure carbon. It will endow the high level of functionality and complexity of the obtained electrode of HCNs [17,18]. Below, we summarized the applications of HCNs for different rechargeable batteries, including LIBs, lithium-sulfur batteries, NIBs, KIBs, and ZABs.
## 3.1 Lithium-ion battery: high-capacity anode
Industrial graphite has long been deemed as the reliable anode materials for commercial LIBs [75–77]. However, graphite anode was limited by its low theoretical specific capacity of $372\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is hard to meet the demand for building high-energy density LIBs. Therefore, it is essential to develop the high-capacity anode materials with considerable lifespan [57,78–80]. MOFs-derived HCNs are drawing increasing research interest as high-capacity anode materials due to their outstanding lithium storage capability originating from the unique hollow architecture and derived nanocomposite capable of providing abundant storage sites [34,35]. A representative work has been recently reported by Zhang and co-workers [35], who showed that core-shell Ndoped HCNs embedded with cobalt nanoparticles could deliver a high reversible capacity for $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ storage. Figure 9a illustrated the as prepared HCNs derived from ZIF-8@ZIF67, which features hollow core-shelled structure with Ndoped carbon and $\mathrm{Co}$ nanoparticles anchored in graphitic carbon as inner and outer shells, respectively. As LIBs anodes, such HCNs could promise a discharge/charge capacity of $1,730/1,000\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ with a stable expanded cycling (Figure 9b). The Co nanoparticles act as the catalyst to facilitate the graphitization of the outer carbon to form such unique $\mathsf{N C@C o-N G C}$ structure. Benefiting from its slight crumpled plane structure (Figure $\mathrm{9c}\dot{}$ ), the $\mathsf{N C@C o-N G C}$ anode is capable of storing more ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ . Despite of the high specific capacity, the initial Coulombic efficiency of $57.5\%$ for this LIBs anode is still too low for practical application.
In addition to pure carbon species, nanocomposites of transition metal oxides or sulfide incorporated in hollow carbon matrix could also be facilely constructed for highcapacity LIBs anode. A recent contribution by Huang et al. [34] reported that the hybrid hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ carbon nanostructures derived from MOFs showed high reversible specific capacity $(1,150\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and cycling stability (Figure 9d, e). The authors ascribed the high performance to the unique structural average of such a hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ carbon nanocomposite including facile access of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions, improved electrical conductivity, abundant active interfacial active sites, and effectively alleviated volume deformation. Notably, the initial Coulombic efficiency of such an HCN anode is ${\sim}50\%$ , which has to be improved for the practical LIBs.
## 3.2 Li-S battery: efficient and stable cathode
Li-S batteries with sulfur as cathode material has long been considered as one of the leading candidates for high-energydensity storage device owing to the low cost and high specific capacity of $1,675\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ of sulfur cathode [81–83]. However, the potential of sulfur cathode is limited by several problems. The fatal one is the formation of lithium polysulfides (LiPS), which are soluble in the electrolyte, leading to the shuttle effect of the LiPS [84–86]. As a result, the reversible capacity of Li-S battery decays rapidly. Besides, sulfur cathode was also challenged by its insulating nature, sluggish reaction kinetics for LiPS conversion, and serious volumetric change during the lithiation/delithiation process [87,88].
Researchers demonstrated that MOFs-derived HCN nanocomposite of hollow carbon/metal compound or hollow carbon/metal was able to effectively resolve the abovementioned problems in Li-S batteries. For example, Jin et al. [37] reported a strategy of constructing interlaced carbon nanotubes threaded hollow $\mathrm{Co_{3}S_{4}/C}$ nanoboxes $\mathrm{(Co_{3}S_{4}/C-}$ CNT) as sulfur host material to build high-performance S cathodes (Figure 10a, b). The obtained hollow $\mathrm{S}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}/\mathrm{C}.$ CNT not only showed a strong capability of LiPS adsorption to suppress the shuttling effect, but could also accelerate the LiPS redox reaction by an outstanding catalytic activity. As a result, the designed hollow $\mathrm{\DeltaS(\mathcal{Q}C o_{3}S_{4}/C}$ -CNT cathode delivered superior electrochemical performance with high capacity, long-term cyclability, and outstanding rate performance. Beside the carbon/metal sulfide, MOFs also emerged as an effective synthetic platform for single metal atom anchored into HCNs, which was effective for suppressing the LiPS shuttling and accelerate the conversion kinetics of LiPS by the single-atom catalysis. Wu and coworkers [36] identified that single atom Zn-decorated HCNs (Zn-HCNs) derived from ZIF-8 were able to strongly adsorb LiPS and kinetically accelerate LiPS conversion by lowing the Gibbs free energy of the LiPS conversion (Figure 10d, e). The S@Zn-HCNs cathodes could ensure a fast and stable lithiation/delithiation for high-rate capability and promising cycling stability (Figure 10f, g).
## 3.3 Sodium-ions battery: high-rate anode
The ever-increasing demand for LIBs speeded up the consumption of the very limited Li resources $(0.0017~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in Earth’s crust), which causes serious concern on its sustainability and cost [89–92]. Owing to the resource abundance $(2.3~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in Earth’s crust) and low cost of Na, sodium-ions batteries (SIBs) have drawn considerable research interest as a promising supplement to LIBs [93–95]. Recent years have witnessed the fast development of promising electrode materials for SIBs [96,97]. However, the anode materials with outstanding rate capability and reliable cycle life are still deficient, which is probable ascribed to the much larger size of $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ $1.02\mathrm{\AA}$ , in contract to $0.76\mathrm{\AA}$ for $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ). The intercalation/deintercalation of $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ would lead to slow electrode kinetics and large volumetric charge during the sodiation/ desodiation process [7,97,98]. Accordingly, reliable anodes for SIBs with high-rate capability and reliable cyclability are in urgent demand [98,99].
In this regard, MOFs-derived HCNs emerged as highly favored choice because their extraordinary capability in enhancing the reaction kinetics and buffering against the structural deformations. A typical contribution reported by Yamauchi and co-workers [22] indicated that the singleshelled HCNs (1S-HCNs) derived from ZIF-8 could be able to promise much improved rate capability and superior cycling stability than the solid one. As shown in Figure 11a, even at a high current density of $15\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , the 1S-HCNs could give a high reversible capacity of $90\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ . The 1S-HCNs also showed outstanding cycling stability ( Figure 11b), suggesting promising potential for its future application in NIBs anode. Besides, Liang et al. [100] demonstrated that a composite anode constructed by $\mathrm{Co_{9}S_{8}/Z n S}$ nanocrystals incorporated in hollow nitrogen-doped carbon nanosheets was able to achieve an obvious pseudocapacitive effect, high sodium diffusion coefficient, and high reversibility for $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ storage (Figure 11c). From the perspective of electrode design, the nitrogen-doped two-dimension hollow carbon nanosheet would not only greatly contribute to the pseudocapacitance effect but also reduce the diffusion distance to facilitate charge transport for a fast diffusion kinetics (Figure 11c, d). Such a hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}/\mathrm{ZnS}@\mathrm{C}$ nanostructure could deliver a high reversible capacity of $529\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ with a superior rate capability of $258.6\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ (Figure 11e). Meanwhile, it has to be pointed out that the initial coulombic efficiencies for both 1S-HCNs $(68.4\%)$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}/\mathrm{ZnS}@\mathrm{C}\left(\right.$ $(71\%)$ are far from satisfaction for its application in the full-cell SIBs.
## 3.4 Potassium-ions battery: stable anode
Although recent years have witnessed the fast development of NIBs beyond Li resource, there is a demand for new energy storage technologies for various applications, such as large-scale energy storage [54,101,102]. Owing to the resource abundance of K element $(1.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in Earth’s crust), KIBs, sharing a similar work mechanism with LIBs and NIBs, are now drawing increasing research interest. Compared with NIBs, KIBs possessed the following unique advantages [103–106]: (1) much lower redox potential of the $\kappa^{+}/\kappa$ $-2.93\mathrm{~V~}$ in contract to the value of $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}/\mathrm{{Na}}}$ $(-2.71\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. standard hydrogen electrode (SHE)), which ensures the possibility of building a high energy density of KIBs; (2) safe intercalation potential of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ in carbon-based anodes $(\sim0.2\:\mathrm{V}$ vs. $\upkappa^{+}/\upkappa$ , in contract to the value of $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ at ${\sim}0\mathrm{v}$ vs. $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}/\mathrm{{Na}}}$ ), which could effective avoid the K metal dendrite formation in high-rate potassiation/depotassiation [107,108]; (3) high electrochemical activity of industrial graphite for $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ storage with a theoretical capacity of $279\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ . Despite their appealing potential, KIBs were troubled by the large size of $\operatorname{K}^{^{+}}$ $\begin{array}{r}{{1.33\mathrm{\AA}}.}\end{array}$ , in contrast to that of $0.68\mathrm{\AA}$ for $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ), which causes serious volumetric deformation of the electrode in potassiation/depotassiation. For example, $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ intercalating into graphite anode for $\mathrm{KC}_{8}$ would lead a large volume expansion of $60\%$ compared with that of $11\%$ for ${\mathrm{LiC}}_{6}$ (Figure 12a, b) [109]. The serious volume deformation of the electrode would lead to the fast capacity decay (Figure 12c) [106]. Therefore, the development of reliable anode materials remains a formidable challenge [109–111]. Undoubtedly, the key point for the development of the KIBs anode lies in dealing with the serious volumetric change issues of the electrode for a stable potassiation/depotassiation.
The MOFs-derived HCNs with robust structure were identified as promising anode material to fight against the volumetric deformations and enable excellent cycling endurance. A recent work by Hong et al. [40] revealed the great advantage of the hollow porous carbon octahedron (HPCO) anode with regard to the cycling stability. Figure 13a showed the TEM image of HPCO, which featured an obvious cavity in the central of the nanoparticles. Figure 13b illustrated the cycling stability of the HPCO for KIBs, which showed no obvious capacity decay upon 3,000 cycles at $\mathrm{~1~A~g~}^{-1}$ , suggesting a highly stable potassiation/depotassiation process. Unfortunately, such a stable KIB anode is still limited by the low Coulombic efficiency of $\sim30\%$ . Another typical contribution by Chen et al. [39] also demonstrated that a hollow nanocomposite of double-shelled Ni-Fe-P/N-doped carbon nanobox $\mathrm{(Ni{-}F e{\mathrm{-}}P/C N)}$ from MOFs could promise stable cyclability for $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ storages (Figure 13c, d). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) analysis of the cycled Ni-FeP/NC anode reveals that the charge-transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$
of the anode becomes lower, indicating the fast electrode kinetics and the good structure stability of the electrode. Despite the stable cycling, we have to point out that such a KIB anode delivered low initial Coulombic efficiency (CE) of $32.2\%$ .
## 3.5 Zn-air battery: high-activity electrocatalyst
ZABs, which employed the $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ from atmosphere as the cathode, emerged as a promising energy storage technology due to its low cost, high safety, and high theoretical energy density $(1,086\mathrm{~W~h~kg}^{-1})$ [112–114]. According to the working mechanism of ZABs (Figure 14a), the air cathode would experience reversible redox reaction of $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ during discharge/charging process [115,116]. In the discharging process, the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) occurred as follows: $\mathrm{O}_{2}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rightarrow4\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ; In the charging process, the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) occurred as follows: $4\mathrm{OH}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{O}_{2}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Therefore, one of the most critical points for the development of high-performance ZABs cathodes lies in the efficient bifunctional electrocatalysis for both OER and ORR [117]. An efficient bifunctional electrocatalysis would ensure small voltage polarization of discharging/charging, high power density, and stable cyclability [117].
Benefiting from its unique compositional chemistry constituted by organic ligands and uniform-distribution inorganic metal nodes, MOFs-derived HCN nanocomposites of transition metal compound anchored in a hollow porous conductive carbon matrix could promise high conceptual and technological potential for efficient bifunctional electrocatalysis for ZABs [23,41,118–120]. Typically, Xu et al. [23] demonstrated that MOFs-derived HCNs, which were constructed by iron-nickel phosphide immobilized on carbon interconnected via the formed carbon nanotubes (FeNiP/
## 4 Summary and perspectives
This review summarized the recent advances on the template-free synthesis of HCNs from MOFs, and discussed their application in rechargeable batteries (Figure 15). As carbon precursor, MOFs with nano-/micro-structure are attracting enormous research interest due to their uniform combinations of organic ligands and inorganic metal nodes in the framework, which provide great opportunities to facilely produce functional HCNs, including the hollow architectures of pure carbon, carbon/metal single atom, carbon/ metal oxide, carbon/metal sulfide, and their hybrid. In order to extricate the HCNs synthesis from the limitation of template routes, persistent efforts were devoted to regulate the chemistry of MOF nanoparticles and subsequently tuned MOFs into the functional HCNs without using extra templates. The reported template-free methods could be classified into five categories, including surface-protected etching, selective etching, ligand exchange, surface-stabilized contraction, and gas-induced inflation. Based on these templatefree protocols, various HCNs could be facilely prepared with control of the features including architectures, shells, cavities, and compositions. The MOFs-derived HCNs with a high level of complexity and functionality have demonstrated significant potential in rechargeable batteries including LIBs, Li-S batteries, NIBs, KIBs, and ZABs. Particularly, MOF-derived HCNs are demonstrated to be highly reliable anode for NIBs and KIBs with superior rate capability and highly stable capability. The exceptional performances were benefited from the fact that HCNs could not only provide abundant storage sites but also fight against the destructive volumetric deformation and sluggish charge diffusion caused by the intercalation/deintercalation of the heavy and large-size $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ or $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ .
Despite the various advantages, several challenges remained for the further development of MOFs-derived HCNs and the rechargeable batteries applications. From the synthesis point of view, the below issues cannot be ignored: (1) Multi-steps and strict operations for the MOF growth cannot be avoided, like surface protection, ligand exchange, and selective etching. (2) The current synthesis of MOF particles largely relies on high-temperature and high-pressure procedures like solvothermal methods, which are high energy-consumption and a formidable challenge for scale-up production. (3) Most of the organic ligands and metal salts for building MOFs are expensive compared with common carbon precursors of resin polymer or biomass. (4) The scalable synthesis of the MOF precursors for HCNs is challenging and the product yield is still far from the demand of industrial applications.
As far as the challenges of electrochemical performances are considered, some key parameters, particularly the low columbic efficiency, are urgently needed to be improved for their practical applications in future. Generally, MOFs-derived HCNs are usually porous with large specific surface area and low tap density, which would consume large amount of irreversible metal-ion $(\mathrm{Li}^{+},\mathrm{Na}^{+},\mathrm{K}^{+})$ induced by the formation a solid electrolyte interphase (SEI), thus leading to the low initial Columbic efficiency for the anode of metalion battery (e.g., LIBs, NIBs, and KIBs). It is reasonable to expect that the precise control on the pore structure or/and designing suitable electrolyte will be an important research subject in MOF-derived HCNs electrode to improve the Columbic efficiency. Moreover, it is of importance to ambiguously clear the relationships between key features of HCNs (e.g., shell thickness, pore size, and the interspacing) and the electrochemical performance (e.g., coulombic efficiency, specific capacity, rate, and cyclability). However, the systematic study on such relationship using a suitable model material is still very rare. We only noticed that Cao et al. [121] reported that the thickness of shell of hard-carbon HCNs would have significant effect on the performance of Na storage when using HCNs with different shell thicknesses of 10, 15, 30, 50 nm as anode for Na-ion batteries. The HCNs with shell thickness of $30\mathrm{nm}$ delivered the best initial coulomb efficiencies, capacity and rate capability for the shellthickness controlled series. In the future study, more efforts should be devoted to better understanding the structureperformance relationship, particularly considering that different kinds of batteries may have different requirements. We expect that this review will spark new endeavors in the development of MOFs-derived HCNs for electrochemical applications including rechargeable battery, supercapacitor, and electrocatalysis.
